#proof[
  The restriction kernels on $Sigma$ are respectively
  $ cal(A)_j - C = \( - 2 - j \, - 2 \) \, wide cal(A)_j - Gamma = \( 4 - j \, 0 \) . $
  Product cohomology on $bb(P)^1 times bb(P)^1$ gives $H^0 = H^1 = 0$ for the first kernel and $H^1 =
    0$ for the second. Adjunction therefore gives the two assertions about restriction.
  By (2.5), the ideals of the two length-$ell$ subschemes in the local surface are
  $ \( y - phi \( z \) \, z^ell \) = \( y \, z^ell \) wide \( ell lt.eq 4 \) . $
  Thus these are the same embedded subscheme, and restrictions of the surface line bundle and its sections to them
    agree. The two surjectivity statements just proved imply equality of evaluation ranks.
  On the elliptic curve, $deg F = 2$, $Z = 3 F + Delta$, and $2 Z = 6 F$.
    These identities give every residual bundle in (2.9). A positive-degree line bundle on an elliptic curve has
    as many sections as its degree; a negative-degree bundle has no section.
    The degree-zero bundle $Delta$ is nontrivial, so both its zeroth and first cohomology vanish.
    Subtracting the residual section dimensions from $h^0 \( Gamma \, N_j \)$ gives the stated ranks.
    In particular, the twelve-dimensional surface system for $j =
    3$ has a two-dimensional kernel on restriction to $Gamma$,
    and the resulting ten-dimensional system injects into its length-twelve evaluation space. ◻
]

#thm([Proposition 2.3.])[
