#proof[
  The existence and contact assertions were established above.
    From $C = \( 8 \, 4 \)$ and $K_C = \( 6 \, 2 \) \|_C$, intersection on the quadric gives
  $ deg H = 4 \, wide deg K_C = 6 dot.op 4 + 2 dot.op 8 = 40 \, wide g \( C \) = 21 . $
  Since $deg Z = 6$, one has $deg L = 10$. The morphism given by $H$ is the first projection,
    restricted to the irreducible curve $C$, hence is finite of degree four.
    Tangency at the marked points identifies its differential there with that of $F \|_Gamma$, which is nonzero.
    Its marked values are the corresponding $F \|_Gamma$-values, already seen not to be all equal.
    Choose a regular fiber away from these finitely many values.
    A determinant-one change of the basis $u$ makes this fiber the zero divisor of $u_1$.
    Such a change preserves $u_1 thin d u_2 - u_2 thin d u_1$ on both curves,
    and hence preserves the quadratic form and every normalization already chosen.
  For $0 lt.eq j lt.eq 3$, the sequence restricting $cal(O)_Sigma \( j \, 0 \)$ to $C$ has kernel
    $cal(O)_Sigma \( j - 8 \, - 4 \)$, with $H^0 = H^1 = 0$ by product cohomology.
    This proves $h^0 \( C \, j H \) = j + 1$. For any line bundle $B$ on $C$ and effective divisor $D$,
    Riemann–Roch and Serre duality give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.13)")
   $ h^0 \( C \, B + D \) - h^0 \( C \, B \) = deg D - "rank"
     (H^0 \( C \, K_C - B \) arrow.r H^0 \( D \, \( K_C - B \) \|_D \)) . $ <curve:dimension-formula>]
  Apply this with $B = j H$ and use the ranks in Lemma 3. Its first three rows give
  $ h^0 \( C \, cal(O)_C \( Z \) \) = 1 \, quad h^0 \( C \, H + 2 Z \) = 2 \, quad h^0 \( C \, 2 H + 2 Z \) = 3 . $
  The inclusions of $H^0 \( C \, H \)$ into the spaces for $H + Z$ and $H + 2 Z$ then give $h^0 \( C \, L \) = 2$.
    The fourth row gives
  $ h^0 \( C \, 3 H + 2 Z \) = h^0 \( C \, 3 H \) + 12 - 10 = 6 . $
  This proves (2.10), and equality of the dimensions in the natural multiplication inclusions proves (2.11).
    Because the pencil $u$ has no base point and $Z$ is reduced,
    the assertion about the common zeros of $r$ follows as well.
  Finally, the divisor sequence for $N = \( k + 1 \) L$, followed by Serre duality,
    shows that (2.12) is surjective if and only if the natural inclusion
  $ H^0 \( C \, \( 3 - k \) L \) arrow.r H^0 \( C \, \( 3 - k \) L + Z \) $
  is an isomorphism. For $k = 2$ this is the inclusion from $L$ to $L + Z = H + 2 Z$,
    between two-dimensional spaces. For $k = 3$ it is the inclusion from $cal(O)_C$ to $cal(O)_C \( Z \)$,
    between one-dimensional spaces. For $k gt.eq 4$ both spaces vanish,
    since the target bundle has degree $10 \( 3 - k \) + 6 lt.eq - 4$.
    The final assertion follows from Lemma 2 and (2.4). ◻
]

For later use, Riemann–Roch and Serre duality also give

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(2.14)")
 $ h^1 \( C \, L \) = h^0 \( C \, 3 L \) = 12 \, wide h^0 \( C \, 4 L \) = 21 . $ <curve:boundary-cohomology>]

Indeed, $h^0 \( C \, L \) - h^1 \( C \, L \) = 10 + 1 - 21 = - 10$ and $K_C - L = 3 L$.

One additional consequence will be useful when removing a two-dimensional obstruction.
  In local frames compatible with $L = H + Z$, with $xi$ represented by a parameter $z$ at a marked point,
  one has $r = z u$ and $r' \( 0 \) = u \( 0 \)$. The resulting six nonzero row vectors $r' \( q_j \)$ span
  $bb(C)^2$: their projective directions are the pencil values $f \( q_j \)$, which are not all equal.
  Changing the frames or the local parameters only rescales each of these rows.

== 3 The trace form and the residue data <trace:section>

The goal of this section is Proposition 7: a meromorphic pair with prescribed poles and two normalized residue
  identities. The trace form translates the quadratic-space data on the elliptic curve into those identities on
  $C$. The comparison must preserve its scalar and sign, since both enter the first gluing obstruction.

Retain the data and the normalizations of Proposition 4. In particular,
  $Omega$ is the fixed logarithmic volume on $Sigma$, its normal-first residue is the nowhere vanishing
  differential $omega$ on $Gamma$, and $4 L tilde.eq K_C$ is the specified adjunction isomorphism. Put

$ theta = 2 L \, wide Lambda = theta + H = 3 H + 2 Z \, wide f : C arrow.r bb(P)^1 . $

For the chosen basis $u = \( u_1 \, u_2 \)$ of the ruling pencil, write $eta_C = u_1 thin d u_2 -
  u_2 thin d u_1 in H^0 \( C \, K_C + 2 H \)$. Although the individual derivatives depend on a frame of $H$,
  this alternating expression does not. We use the analogous notation $eta_Gamma$ on $Gamma$.

=== 3.1 A two-dimensional quotient of a perfect trace form <a-two-dimensional-quotient-of-a-perfect-trace-form>

The pairing below is a concrete instance of finite-morphism duality,
  as developed by Grothendieck and presented in Hartshorne’s #emph[Residues and Duality] [18] Chapter III, §6.
  We check its local perfection directly so that the trace normalization remains explicit.

#thm([Lemma 3.1.])[
  #emph[The rank-four vector bundle $E = f_(*) Lambda$ has a perfect symmetric pairing
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.1)")
 $ E ⊗ E arrow.r cal(O)_(bb(P)^1) \, wide \( T_1 \, T_2 \) mapsto "Tr"_(C \/ bb(P)^1) #h(-0.16667em)
   (frac(T_1 T_2, eta_C)) . $ <trace:form>]

 Its splitting type is $\( 3 \, 0 \, 0 \, - 3 \)$. Multiplication by $xi^2$ identifies $cal(O)_(bb(P)^1) \( 3 \)$
   with its positive line subbundle $cal(P)$. The form on global sections has radical $xi^2 H^0 \( C \, 3 H \)$,
   and induces a nondegenerate symmetric form $chevron.l med \, med chevron.r$ on
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.2)")
 $ W = H^0 \( C \, Lambda \) \/ xi^2 H^0 \( C \, 3 H \) \, wide dim W = 2 . $ <trace:W>]

 For every $x in bb(P)^1$, evaluation identifies this quadratic space with $\( cal(P)^perp \/ cal(P) \)_x$.]
  #emph[Suppose $q in Z$, and choose local frames $frak(f)$ of $H$ and $tau$ of $Lambda$,
    and a parameter $z$ at $q$. Write, using the fixed isomorphism $2 Lambda tilde.eq K_C + 2 H$,
$ tau^2 = s \( z \) thin d z thin frak(f)^2 \, wide eta_C = cal(E) \( z \) thin d z thin frak(f)^2 . $

 Coefficient evaluation $ell_q : T mapsto \( T \/ tau \) \( q \)$ descends to $W$. Its square in the dual form is
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.3)")
 $ ell_q^2 = frac(cal(E) \( q \), s \( q \)) . $ <trace:dual-evaluation>]

 In particular, it is nonzero and nonisotropic.]
] <trace:pairing>

#proof[
  The morphism $f$ is finite and flat of degree four. The identity $2 Lambda = K_C +
    2 H$ identifies $Lambda^2$ with the relative dualizing bundle.
    We verify directly the normalization and perfection of its trace pairing.
    Locally at a ramification point one can choose parameters with $x =
    w^e$ on a single component over a target disk. A frame of $H$ pulled back from that disk can be chosen so that
    $eta_C = d x thin frak(f)^2$. If $tau^2 = a \( w \) thin d w thin frak(f)^2$, with $a$ a unit, the local pairing is
  $ \( b \, c \) mapsto "Tr" #h(-0.16667em) (frac(a \( w \) b c, e w^(e - 1))) . $
  On the basis $1 \, w \, dots.h \, w^(e - 1)$, the pairing without the unit $a$ has matrix
  $ "Tr" #h(-0.16667em) (frac(w^(i + j), e w^(e - 1))) =
    cases(delim: "{", 1 & i + j = e - 1 \,, 0 & i + j eq.not e - 1 \,) wide 0 lt.eq i \, j < e . $
  It is therefore holomorphic and perfect. Multiplication by $a$ is an automorphism,
    and taking the direct sum over the points above the target disk proves the assertion.
    On an unramified fiber the trace is the sum of its four contributions; there is no division by the degree.
  By the splitting theorem for vector bundles on $bb(P)^1$ [17] §2, Theorem 2.1 and Corollary,
    $E$ is a direct sum of line bundles. Self-duality implies that its splitting degrees occur in opposite pairs.
    Proposition 4 gives
  $ h^0 \( E \( - 1 \) \) = h^0 \( theta \) = 3 \, wide h^0 \( E \( - 2 \) \) = h^0 \( H + 2 Z \) = 2 . $
  A positive splitting degree $a$ contributes $a$ and $a - 1$, respectively, to these two numbers,
    and all nonpositive degrees contribute zero. There is consequently exactly one positive degree, equal to three. Thus
  $ E tilde.eq cal(O) \( 3 \) xor cal(O)^(xor 2) xor cal(O) \( - 3 \) . $
  The nonzero map $cal(O) \( 3 \) arrow.r E$ obtained from multiplication by $xi^2$ has zero components in the
    three lower-degree summands. Its component in $cal(O) \( 3 \)$ is a nonzero constant.
    Hence its image $cal(P)$ is the entire positive summand, and is a subbundle,
    including over the images of the points of $Z$. The injective pullback $H^0 \( bb(P)^1 \, cal(O) \( 3 \) \)
    arrow.r H^0 \( C \, 3 H \)$ is an isomorphism, since both spaces have dimension four.
    Thus $H^0 \( cal(P) \) = xi^2 H^0 \( C \, 3 H \)$.
  The line $cal(P)$ is isotropic, since $"Hom" \( cal(O) \( 6 \) \, cal(O) \) = 0$.
    Likewise it pairs trivially with the two degree-zero summands. Perfection gives
  $ cal(P)^perp tilde.eq cal(O) \( 3 \) xor cal(O)^(xor 2) \, wide cal(P)^perp \/ cal(P) tilde.eq cal(O)^(xor 2) \, $
  with a nondegenerate constant form on the quotient. All global sections of $E$ lie in $cal(P)^perp$,
    and $H^1 \( cal(P) \) = 0$. Taking sections proves the assertions about $W$ and its fibers.
  At $q in Z$, evaluation annihilates $cal(P)_(f \( q \))$,
    because the map defining that line is multiplication by $xi^2$.
    The finite fiber algebra is a product of its Artin-local factors,
    and the trace pairing is the orthogonal sum of their pairings.
    Since $f$ is unramified at $q$, the factor at $q$ is one-dimensional,
    with form coefficient $s \( q \) \/ cal(E) \( q \)$. Evaluation at $q$ vanishes on the other factors,
    whether or not they are ramified. The metric dual of coefficient evaluation thus has square $cal(E) \( q \) \/
    s \( q \)$ in $E_(f \( q \))$. It lies in $cal(P)_(f \( q \))^perp$,
    because the evaluation kills $cal(P)_(f \( q \))$. Passing to the orthogonal quotient preserves this square
    and gives the functional on $W$. Finally $cal(E) \( q \) eq.not 0$ by the unramifiedness in Proposition 4. ◻
]

We write a dot for the dual bilinear form on $W^(*)$, so that $lambda^2 = lambda dot.op lambda$.
  The distinction between this dual form and the form on $W$ accounts for the reciprocal in (3.3).

=== 3.2 Identification by full principal parts <identification-by-full-principal-parts>

The preceding lemma gives a quadratic space on $C$. We now identify it with the one on $Gamma$ used to choose the
  marked points. The principal-part exact sequence, together with Serre duality [26] §3, Theorem 4; §4, Theorem 5,
  characterizes realizable principal parts by their residue pairings with global adjoint sections.
  We first compare both coefficients of each double pole to identify the two spaces.
  We then use the leading coefficients to determine the scalar and sign of the quadratic form under that identification.

#thm([Lemma 3.2.])[
  #emph[There is an isomorphism
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.4)")
 $ iota : W arrow.r^(med tilde.op med) H^0 \( Gamma \, Phi \) $ <trace:iota>]

 that identifies the full principal parts of $T \/ xi^2$ on $C$ with those of $iota \( \[ T \] \) \/
   beta^2$ on $Gamma$ through residue duality. It identifies the projective evaluations at corresponding points of
   $Z$. Moreover, its dual
$ iota^(*) : H^0 \( Gamma \, Phi \)^(*) arrow.r W^(*) $

 is an isometry for the quadratic form $Q$ of Proposition 4 and the dual trace form.]
] <trace:principal-parts>

#proof[
  Set $N_Sigma = Gamma + F$, $N_Gamma = N_Sigma \|_Gamma$, and $D = 2 Z$.
    Fourth-order contact identifies $D$ on $C$ and on $Gamma$ as the same embedded length-twelve subscheme of
