=== 3.3 Meromorphic sections with the required residues <meromorphic-sections-with-the-required-residues>

The isometry now turns the six chosen vectors into prescribed pole coefficients on $C$.
  A global residue calculation will show that these poles force the required local determinant normalization;
  no further choice of regular parts will be needed. We pass from the ambient coordinates used for adjunction to
  coordinates adapted to $4 L tilde.eq K_C$. On a small disk centered at $q_j$ choose a parameter $z_j$ and a
  frame $ell_j^(upright("fr"))$ of $L$ whose fourth power maps to $d z_j$.
  Such a frame exists by taking a holomorphic fourth root of a unit. Set

$ a_j = xi \/ z_j \, wide frak(f)_j = ell_j^(upright("fr")) \/ a_j \, wide
  tau_j = \( ell_j^(upright("fr")) \)^2 frak(f)_j . $

These are frames of $cal(O)_C \( Z \)$, $H$, and $Lambda$, respectively.
  If $r = xi u$, its coefficient row in $ell_j^(upright("fr"))$ satisfies $r = z_j u$,
  where here $u$ denotes its coefficient row in $frak(f)_j$.
  The fiber-linear function $t_j$ on $L^(- 1)$ corresponding to $ell_j^(upright("fr"))$ gives compatible split
  coordinates $\( t_j \, z_j \)$. Let $l_j in W^(*)$ be coefficient evaluation in $tau_j$.
  Since $tau_j^2 = d z_j thin frak(f)_j^2$, Lemma 5 reads in these coordinates as

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.12)")
 $ l_j^2 = \( u_1 u'_2 - u_2 u'_1 \) \( 0 \) . $ <trace:compatible-norm>]

Primes in the rest of this section refer to $z_j$-derivatives on the disk under consideration.

#thm([Proposition 3.3.])[
  (Residue data).  #emph[For the curve, line bundles, fixed fourth-root isomorphism,
    and six vectors $w_j in H^0 \( Gamma \, Phi \)^(*)$ of Proposition 4,
    one can choose the basis $u$ with unchanged determinant so that $\( u_1 \)_0$ is an unramified fiber of $f$
    disjoint from $Z$. Set $r = xi u$ and define
$ v_j^(*) = iota^(*) w_j in W^(*) \, wide V = sum_(j = 1)^6 v_j^(*) . $

 For any mutually disjoint compatible coordinate disks as above there are nonzero constants $c_j$ and a global pair
$ P = \( P_1 \, P_2 \) in H^0 \( C \, theta + Z \)^(xor 2) $

 with the following properties. First,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.13)")
 $ c_j l_j = v_j^(*) \, wide V^2 = 90 = 9 deg L \, wide \( v_j^(*) \)^2 + v_j^(*) dot.op V =
   1 . $ <trace:vector-identities>]

 On disk $j$, using coefficient rows in the indicated frames, put
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.14)")
 $ m_j = c_j / z_j \, wide R_j = m_j r \, wide W_j = R_j \( 0 \) and R'_j \( 0 \) \, wide p_j = P -
   m_j r' . $ <trace:local-data>]

 The pair $P$ has principal part $R_j \( 0 \) \/ z_j$ in the frame $\( ell_j^(upright("fr")) \)^2$ of $theta$;
   the rows $R_j \, p_j$ are holomorphic on the disk, and $R_j \( 0 \) eq.not 0$. Moreover,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.15)")
 $ W_j = \( v_j^(*) \)^2 \, wide p_j \( 0 \) and R_j \( 0 \) = 1 \, wide sum_(j = 1)^6 \( W_j - 1 \) = -
   9 deg L . $ <trace:residue-identities>]

 Globally,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.16)")
 $ J = u and P in H^0 \( C \, Lambda \) \, wide chevron.l \[ J \] \, \[ T \] chevron.r = - V \( \[ T \] \) quad
   upright("for every ") T in H^0 \( C \, Lambda \) . $ <trace:J>]

 Changing $P$ by a holomorphic pair of sections of $theta$ does not change $\[ J \]$.
   The pole prescription is consistent under changes of compatible coordinates.]
] <prop:residue-data>

#proof[
  A determinant-one change of basis preserves $eta_C$ and $eta_Gamma$, and hence all the forms already fixed.
    Avoiding the finitely many branch values and the image of $Z$,
    we may arrange that the zero divisor of $u_1$ is the required fiber.
    The isometry of Lemma 6 transports the equations for $w_j$ to (3.13).
    It also transports their evaluation directions. Since each $l_j$ is nonzero,
    there is a unique nonzero $c_j$ with $c_j l_j = v_j^(*)$.
    Locally $R_j = c_j u$, so it is holomorphic and nonzero at the center, and (3.12) gives
  $ R_j \( 0 \) and R'_j \( 0 \) = c_j^2 l_j^2 = \( v_j^(*) \)^2 . $
  Every prescribed set of simple principal parts of $theta$ at $Z$ is realizable by a section of $theta + Z$.
    Indeed, the obstruction in $H^1 \( C \, theta \)$ pairs, by Serre duality, with $H^0 \( C \, K_C - theta \) =
    H^0 \( C \, theta \)$ by the sum of residues. All these test sections vanish at $Z$, by Proposition 4.
    Their residue against any simple principal part is therefore zero.
    Apply this separately to the two entries to obtain $P$ with the stated pole parts.
    Since $r = z_j u$, the row $m_j r'$ has exactly the same simple pole part as $P$; thus $p_j$ is holomorphic.
  The simple pole of $P$ at $q_j$ is proportional to $u \( q_j \)$.
    Its wedge with $u$ has zero residue coefficient, so $J = u and P$ is holomorphic there;
    away from $Z$ it is already holomorphic. Hence it is a global section of $Lambda$.
    To compute its class, fix $T in H^0 \( C \, Lambda \)$ and consider the global meromorphic canonical differential
  $ nu = frac(P_1 T, u_1) \, wide theta + \( theta + H \) - H = 2 theta = K_C . $
  Its only possible poles lie in $Z$ and in the simple zero fiber of $u_1$.
    At $q_j$, the compatible frames make the canonical product exactly multiplication of coefficients followed by
    $d z_j$, and therefore
  $ "Res"_(q_j) nu = c_j l_j \( T \) . $
  At a zero $q$ of $u_1$, the section $u_2$ is nonzero and
  $ J \( q \) = - u_2 \( q \) P_1 \( q \) \, wide eta_C \( q \) = - u_2 \( q \) thin d u_1 \( q \) . $
  Both expressions have the same minus sign. In a local parameter, the residue is consequently
  $ "Res"_q nu = (frac(J T, eta_C)) \( q \) . $
  The zero fiber is unramified, so the sum of these four residues is the value of $"Tr" \( J T \/ eta_C \)$ there.
    This trace is a global holomorphic function on $bb(P)^1$ by Lemma 5, hence is constant. The residue theorem gives
  $ chevron.l \[ J \] \, \[ T \] chevron.r = - sum_j c_j l_j \( T \) = - V \( \[ T \] \) \, $
  which proves (3.16) with the stated sign.
  On a fixed disk suppress the subscript $j$. Since $r = z u$,
  $ P = p + c / z u + c u' \, wide c J = R and p + R and R' . $
  In particular,
  $ c_j l_j \( J \) = R_j \( 0 \) and p_j \( 0 \) + W_j . $
  On the other hand, the metric identification in (3.16) gives
  $ c_j l_j \( J \) = - v_j^(*) dot.op V = W_j - 1 . $
  Thus $R_j \( 0 \) and p_j \( 0 \) = - 1$, or equivalently $p_j \( 0 \) and R_j \( 0 \) = 1$.
    Summing $W_j - 1 = - v_j^(*) dot.op V$ proves the last identity in (3.15). Notice also the useful local expansion
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(3.17)")
   $ P = frac(R_j \( 0 \), z_j) + p_j \( 0 \) + 2 R'_j \( 0 \) + O \( z_j \) . $ <trace:P-constant>]
  If $P$ is changed by a holomorphic pair $S$, the equality $H^0 \( C \, theta \) =
    xi^2 H^0 \( C \, 2 H \)$ shows that $u and S in xi^2 H^0 \( C \, 3 H \)$; thus $\[ J \]$ is unchanged.
  For completeness, let $zeta = psi \( z \)$ be another centered parameter,
    and let the corresponding compatible frame of $L$ be $g \( z \) ell^(upright("fr"))$.
    Compatibility says $g^4 = psi'$. The associated changes of the other frames are
  $ a_(upright("new")) = frac(z, psi \( z \)) a \, wide frak(f)_(upright("new")) =
    g frac(psi \( z \), z) frak(f) \, wide tau_(upright("new")) = g^3 frac(psi \( z \), z) tau . $
  Writing $g_0 = g \( 0 \)$, it follows that $l_(upright("new")) = g_0^(- 7) l$,
    so the same vector $v^(*)$ prescribes $c_(upright("new")) = g_0^7 c$. Also
  $ r_(upright("new")) = g^(- 1) r \, wide R_(upright("new")) \( 0 \) = g_0^2 R \( 0 \) . $
  The coefficient row of the global $theta$-section transforms as $P_(upright("new")) = g^(- 2) P$.
    Its coefficient of $zeta^(- 1)$ is $psi' \( 0 \) g_0^(- 2) R \( 0 \) = g_0^2 R \( 0 \)$,
    exactly the newly prescribed $R_(upright("new")) \( 0 \)$.
    Thus the same global $P$ realizes the new prescription. The row $R_(upright("new"))$ is a scalar multiple of
    $R$, with scalar $g_0^2$ at the center; differentiating with respect to $zeta$ shows $W_(upright("new")) =
    g_0^4 W \/ psi' \( 0 \) = W$. Finally, subtracting $m_(upright("new")) thin d r_(upright("new")) \/ d
    zeta$ from $P_(upright("new"))$ shows that $p_(upright("new")) \( 0 \) -
    g_0^(- 2) p \( 0 \)$ is proportional to $R \( 0 \)$. Hence $p_(upright("new")) \( 0 \) and
    R_(upright("new")) \( 0 \) = p \( 0 \) and R \( 0 \)$. All the local conclusions are therefore compatible with
    the fixed fourth-root structure. ◻
]

== 4 Gluing the vector-field jets <jets:section>

We use the curve and sections of Proposition 4, with the normalization supplied by Proposition 7.
  Thus $L^4 tilde.eq K_C$, $deg L = 10$, and $r = \( r_1 \, r_2 \) =
  xi \( u_1 \, u_2 \)$ is a basis of $H^0 \( C \, L \)$ with simple common zero divisor $Z = q_1 + dots.h.c +
  q_6$. For rows $X \, Y$ put $X and Y = X_1 Y_2 - X_2 Y_1$.
  In the compatible coordinates at $q_j$, the preceding proposition gives

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.1)")
 $ m_j = c_j / z \, wide R_j = m_j r \, wide p = P - m_j r' \, wide p \( 0 \) and R_j \( 0 \) = 1 \,\
sum_(j = 1)^6 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] W_j - 1 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] = - 9 deg L \, wide W_j =
  R_j \( 0 \) and R'_j \( 0 \) . $ <jets:residue-input>]

Here $P$ is a meromorphic row of sections of $L^2$, holomorphic off $Z$,
  and $p$ and $R_j$ are holomorphic on the disk in question.
  The projective directions of $r' \( q_j \)$ are not all equal.
  Primes always denote differentiation in that disk’s base coordinate.
  Our task is to choose finitely many neighborhood transitions and compatible vector-field coefficients whose
  determinant has the required leading term. We first solve the exceptional low-order obstruction,
  then extend the fields and their determinant together. Proposition 10 gives the finite jet needed for
  holomorphic lifting.

=== 4.1 Coordinates, transitions, and obstruction spaces <coordinates-transitions-and-obstruction-spaces>

On the total space of $L^(- 1)$ choose a base coordinate $z$ and a fiber-linear equation $t$ of the zero section
  such that $t^4$ corresponds to $d z$ under $L^4 tilde.eq K_C$. Two such choices satisfy

$ tilde(z) = psi \( z \) \, wide tilde(t) = g \( z \) t \, wide g^4 = psi' . $

Put $E_t = t partial_t$. The meromorphic two-form

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.2)")
 $ sigma = t^(- 4) frac(d t, t) and d z = t^(- 5) d t and d z $ <jets:volume>]

is invariant under these split changes of coordinates. We seek rows of coefficients $A_k \, B_k$ defining

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.3)")
 $ v_i = t #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] A_i \( t \, z \) E_t +
   B_i \( t \, z \) partial_z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
A = sum_(k gt.eq 0) A_k t^k \, quad B = sum_(k gt.eq 0) B_k t^k \, wide A_0 = r \, quad B_0 = 0 . $ <jets:fields>]

The subscripts $k$ label orders; subscripts $i \, j in { 1 \, 2 }$ label entries of a row. In particular,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.4)")
 $ sigma \( v_1 \, v_2 \) = t^(- 2) \( A and B \) \, wide v_1 and v_2 = t^2 \( A and B \) E_t and
   partial_z . $ <jets:determinant>]

For a term $t^(k + 1) \( A_k E_t + B_k partial_z \)$, a split coordinate change gives, with $a = \( log g \)'$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.5)")
 $ tilde(A)_k = g^(- \( k + 1 \)) \( A_k + a B_k \) \, wide tilde(B)_k = g^(- \( k + 1 \)) psi' B_k =
   g^(3 - k) B_k . $ <jets:split-coefficients>]

Consequently the horizontal coefficient has type $L^(k - 3)$,
  and the difference of two vertical lifts of a fixed horizontal coefficient has type $L^(k + 1)$.
  More precisely, the sheaf of homogeneous terms of this order is an extension

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.6)")
 $ 0 arrow.r L^(k + 1) arrow.r cal(E)_k arrow.r L^(k - 3) arrow.r 0 . $ <jets:homogeneous-extension>]

This also specifies how rows written in local coordinates are interpreted on a chart without a global coordinate
  or frame.

Take the cover consisting of $U_0 = C \\ Z$ and six mutually disjoint disks $U_j$ about the $q_j$.
  The positive-degree divisor $Z$ has a very ample multiple;
  the corresponding projective embedding realizes $U_0$ as a closed algebraic subvariety of the affine complement
  of a hyperplane. Its analytification is therefore Stein. The disks and annular intersections are Stein as well.
  Thus horizontal sections admit holomorphic vertical lifts on each chart, by (4.6) and Cartan’s Theorem B [5] §4,
  Theorem B. Cousin problems on this cover compute the indicated first cohomology groups.
  Equivalently, one may shrink to a cover with annular intersections.
  For an overlap differential we use the contour residue $frac(1, 2 pi i) integral
  omega$ around the missing point, oriented positively in the disk coordinate.
  The Serre-duality obstruction to a splitting is the sum of these residues after multiplication by a dual
  section. This formulation allows overlap functions holomorphic on annuli;
  they need not be meromorphic at the omitted centers.

We modify the disk-to-outside identifications by flows of the vector fields

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.7)")
 $ D = t^k \( b E_t + h partial_z \) \, wide k > 0 \, $ <jets:generator>]

where $b \, h$ are holomorphic on the overlap in the disk coordinate.
  Use the time-one flow of $- D$, so that its action on vector fields is $exp \( "ad" D \)$.
  Successive modifications are applied to rows already expressed on the outside;
  all formulas below are relative to the original split comparison.
  Every generator vanishes on the zero section and changes its equation only by $t mapsto t + O \( t^2 \)$,
  so the zero section and its conormal bundle are preserved.

#thm([Lemma 4.1.])[
  #emph[For $D$ as in (4.7) and $V = t^(ell + 1) \( A E_t + B partial_z \)$ one has
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.8)")
 $ \[ D \, V \] = t^(k + ell + 1) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] & \( \( ell + 1 - k \) b A + h A' - B b' \) E_t\
 & + \( \( ell + 1 \) b B + h B' - k h A - B h' \) partial_z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <jets:general-bracket>]

 Moreover,
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.9)")
 $ cal(L)_D sigma = t^k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( k - 4 \) b +
   h' #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] sigma . $ <jets:divergence>]

 In particular, for $k eq.not 4$ the choice $b = h' \/ \( 4 - k \)$ preserves $sigma$ exactly.
   The first effect of an order-$k$ modification on (4.3) is
#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.10)")
 $ Delta A_k = \( 1 - k \) b r + h r' \, wide Delta B_k = - k h r . $ <jets:leading-action>]

]
] <jets:bracket>

#proof[
  Use $E_t \( t^a \) = a t^a$ and $\[ E_t \, partial_z \] = 0$ in the two components of the bracket.
    For the divergence, the coefficient of $d t and d z$ in $sigma$ is $t^(- 5)$, so
  $ "div"_sigma D = t^5 (partial_t \( t^(k - 4) b \) + partial_z \( t^(k - 5) h \)) = t^k \( \( k - 4 \) b + h' \) . $
  Setting $ell = 0$, $A = r$, $B = 0$ gives the last formula. ◻
]

All calculations can initially be made with finite Taylor series.
  They nevertheless prescribe actual transition germs: on a compact subannulus every $D$ in (4.7) is holomorphic
  near its zero section and vanishes there, so its time-one flow and inverse exist after shrinking in the
  transverse direction. A finite composition has the computed Taylor series.
  There are no intersections between different disk charts, hence no additional triple-overlap equations.
  The passage from these germs to a Hausdorff surface neighborhood will be made in the next section.

=== 4.2 The first two orders <the-first-two-orders>

At order one use $h = m = m_j$, $b = m' \/ 3$ on the $j$th overlap, and choose

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.11)")
 $ & A_1 & B_1\
U_j & p & m r\
U_0 & P & 0 . $ <jets:first-order>]

By (4.10), the first transition adds $\( m r' \, - m r \)$ to the inside row, giving exactly $\( P \, 0 \)$.
  The degree-one coefficient of $A and B$ is zero on both charts.

At order two write $U = B_2$. To give the determinant its required leading coefficient, solve on each chart

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.12)")
 $ r and U = 1 - A_1 and B_1 . $ <jets:second-horizontal>]

On a disk write $r = z rho$, with $rho$ nowhere zero as a row after shrinking.
  By (4.1), the right side of (4.12) vanishes at $z = 0$, so division by $z$ reduces it to a surjective wedge map
  with $rho$. On $U_0$ the row $r$ has no common zero, and the corresponding surjection of coherent sheaves is
  surjective on sections by Steinness. Thus the required holomorphic horizontal rows exist.
  After the first transition, their horizontal difference on an overlap wedges to zero with $r$:
  the determinant target is compatible because that transition preserves $sigma$.
  The difference is therefore a unique holomorphic multiple of $r$.
  An order-two transition with $b = h' \/ 2$ removes it, since its horizontal effect is $- 2 h r$.
  Keep the chosen outside row $U$ fixed throughout this matching.

It remains to match the vertical lifts $A_2$. The obstruction belongs to $H^1 \( C \, L^3 \)^2$.
  Its four Serre-duality tests use $r_1 \, r_2$, a basis of $H^0 \( C \, K_C ⊗ L^(- 3) \) = H^0 \( C \, L \)$,
  against the two entries of the vertical difference. The following calculation evaluates all four.

#thm([Lemma 4.2.])[
  #emph[The order-two vertical obstruction vanishes.]
] <jets:second-obstruction>

#proof[
  Write $D^(\( 2 \)) = A_2$; this notation denotes the vertical row, not a transition generator.
    For $i \, j in { 1 \, 2 }$ set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.13)")
   $ Psi_(i j) = (r_i D_j^(\( 2 \)) + 1 / 4 r'_i U_j + 3 / 4 r'_j U_i) d z . $ <jets:psi>]
  Once the horizontal rows have matched, the difference of these expressions for the transported inside lift and
    the outside lift is exactly $r_i$ times the $j$th vertical difference, multiplied by $d z$.
    The derivative terms therefore change neither the obstruction nor its four tests.
  First compute the total for the outside lift. Put $epsilon.alt_12 = 1$, $epsilon.alt_21 = - 1$,
    and $epsilon.alt_11 = epsilon.alt_22 = 0$. Under a split change with $a = \( log g \)'$, formula (4.5) gives
  $ tilde(r)_i = g^(- 1) r_i \, wide partial_(tilde(z)) tilde(r)_i = g^(- 5) \( r'_i - a r_i \) \, wide
    tilde(U)_i = g U_i \, wide tilde(D)_i^(\( 2 \)) = g^(- 3) \( D_i^(\( 2 \)) + a U_i \) . $
  Substituting in (4.13), and expressing both differentials in the old coordinate, yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.14)")
   $ tilde(Psi)_(i j) - Psi_(i j) = 3 / 4 \( r_i U_j - r_j U_i \) d log g = 3 /
     4 epsilon.alt_(i j) thin d log g quad upright("on ") U_0 . $ <jets:psi-transformation>]
  The last equality uses $r and U = 1$ on the outside. Use the section $r_1 = xi u_1$ of $L$.
    Its coefficient transforms by $tilde(r)_1 = g^(- 1) r_1$, so $Psi_(i j) + 3 /
    4 epsilon.alt_(i j) d log r_1$ is an intrinsic differential on $U_0$.
    There it has precisely the four possible simple poles at the reduced zero fiber of $u_1$, each of residue $3 /
    4 epsilon.alt_(i j)$. The residue theorem on the complement of small disks therefore gives total contour
    residue $- 3 epsilon.alt_(i j)$ around $Z$ for this intrinsic differential.
    At each of the six points of $Z$, the section $r_1$ has a simple zero.
    Subtracting these six logarithmic residues yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.15)")
   $ sum_(q in Z) "Res"_q Psi_(i j)^(upright("out")) = - 3 / 4 epsilon.alt_(i j) deg L . $ <jets:outside-residue>]
  Only contour integrals at $Z$ were used, so this argument does not assume meromorphic extension of the outside lifts.
  Next compute the transported inside total. Before transport, $Psi_(i j)$ is holomorphic on the disk.
    The order-two transition changes its rows by
  $ delta D^(\( 2 \)) = h r' - 1 / 2 h' r \, wide delta U = - 2 h r . $
  In (4.13) this is the exact differential
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.16)")
   $ delta Psi_(i j) = - 1 / 2 d \( h r_i r_j \) \, $ <jets:psi-exact>]
  so it has zero contour residue. It suffices to calculate the effect of the order-one transition,
    including its second exponential term.
  The bracket of $t \( m' E_t \/ 3 + m partial_z \)$ with the inside order-one term
    $t^2 \( p E_t + m r partial_z \)$ contributes
  $ (1 / 3 m' p + m p' - 1 / 3 m m'' r \, 2 / 3 m m' r + m^2 r' - m p) $
  to $\( A_2 \, U \)$. Half of its second bracket with the leading term $t r E_t$ contributes
  $ (2 / 3 m m' r' + 1 / 2 m^2 r'' + 1 / 6 m m'' r \, - 1 / 3 m m' r - m^2 r') . $
  Thus the full effect is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.17)")
   $ Delta A_2 & = 1 / 3 m' p + m p' + 2 / 3 m m' r' + 1 / 2 m^2 r'' - 1 / 6 m m'' r \,\
Delta U & = - m p + 1 / 3 m m' r . $ <jets:second-expansion>]
  The $m^2 r'$ horizontal terms cancel in this sum.
  Fix one disk and abbreviate $R = m r$, $m = c \/ z$, so $r = z R \/ c$. Substitution in (4.17) gives
  $ Delta A_2 & = - frac(c R, z^3) + frac(c R', 3 z^2) + frac(c R'', 2 z) - frac(c p, 3 z^2) + frac(c p', z) \,\
Delta U & = - frac(c p, z) - frac(c R, 3 z^2) . $
  It follows, with every coefficient on the right evaluated at $0$, that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.18)")
   $ F_(i j) := "Res"_0 \( r_i Delta A_(2 \, j) thin d z \) & = - 1 / 3 R_i p_j - 2 / 3 R_i R'_j - R'_i R_j \,\
H_(i j) := "Res"_0 \( r'_i Delta U_j thin d z \) & = - R_i p_j - 1 / 3 R_i R'_j - 2 /
  3 R'_i R_j . $ <jets:laurent-residues>]
  For example, the double pole $- R_i R_j \/ z^2$ in the first expression contributes $- R'_i R_j -
    R_i R'_j$ to its residue; this accounts for both derivative terms.
    The complete residue of the change in (4.13) is consequently
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.19)")
   $ T_(i j) & = F_(i j) + 1 / 4 H_(i j) + 3 / 4 H_(j i)\
 & = - 7 / 12 R_i p_j - 9 / 12 R_j p_i - 5 / 4 R_i R'_j - 17 / 12 R'_i R_j . $ <jets:full-residue-matrix>]
  Decompose this matrix into half its sum and half its difference with its transpose. Directly from (4.19),
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.20)")
   $ T_(i j)^(upright("sym")) & = - 2 / 3 #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] R_i p_j + R_j p_i + 2 R_i R'_j +
     2 R'_i R_j #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \,\
T_(i j)^(upright("alt")) & = 1 /
  12 epsilon.alt_(i j) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] \( R and p \) \( 0 \) + \( R and R' \) \( 0 \)
  #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] . $ <jets:symmetric-alternating>]
  To identify the symmetric term intrinsically, observe that
  $ P = p + m r' = frac(R \( 0 \), z) + p \( 0 \) + 2 R' \( 0 \) + O \( z \) . $
  Since $P_i P_j$ is a meromorphic section of $L^4 = K_C$, its local expression is $P_i P_j thin d z$, and
  $ "Res"_0 \( P_i P_j thin d z \) = R_i p_j + R_j p_i + 2 R_i R'_j + 2 R'_i R_j . $
  Thus the sum of every symmetric entry in (4.20) is zero, by the residue theorem on $C$.
    For the alternating entry, (4.1) gives $R \( 0 \) and p \( 0 \) = - 1$ and therefore
  $ sum_(q in Z) T_(i j)^(upright("alt")) = 1 / 12 epsilon.alt_(i j) sum_(nu = 1)^6 \( W_nu - 1 \) = - 3 /
    4 epsilon.alt_(i j) deg L . $
  In particular, the sums of the four entries $\( 11 \) \, \( 12 \) \, \( 21 \) \, \( 22 \)$ of the transported
    inside matrix are respectively
  $ 0 \, wide - 3 / 4 deg L \, wide 3 / 4 deg L \, wide 0 . $
  They coincide with the four outside totals in (4.15). Hence every Serre-duality pairing of the vertical
    difference vanishes. The corresponding class in $H^1 \( C \, L^3 \)^2$ is zero,
    so holomorphic vertical corrections on the charts complete the order-two matching. ◻
]

=== 4.3 Higher orders and determinant targets <higher-orders-and-determinant-targets>

From order three onward, each step extends both the fields and the determinant they are required to have.
  Write the determinant equation as

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.21)")
 $ A and B = t^2 G \, wide G = 1 + G_1 t + G_2 t^2 + dots.h.c . $ <jets:target-equation>]

The field equation in degree $k$ uses $G_(k - 2)$. To compare the next horizontal rows,
  this target coefficient must already be compatible on the overlaps.
  We therefore choose each target coefficient before imposing its field equation,
  and keep track of these two extensions separately.

The target is the local bivector $G sigma^(- 1)$. On a realization $Y$, with $cal(I) =
  cal(O)_Y \( - C \)$ and $cal(F) = cal(T)_Y \( - log C \)$, the relevant line bundle is

$ cal(M) = cal(I)^4 det cal(F) = cal(I)^5 det cal(T)_Y \, wide cal(M) \|_C = L^4 ⊗ cal(T)_C tilde.eq cal(O)_C . $

Its local frame is $sigma^(- 1)$, and its degree-$k$ associated graded is $L^k$.
  Only its leading trivialization on $C$ is fixed initially;
  the successive targets extend that section through finite orders.
  After transitions cease to preserve $sigma$, $G$ is therefore not an invariant scalar function.
  To be explicit, if $q = "div"_sigma D$, then

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.22)")
 $ \[ D \, G sigma^(- 1) \] = \( D \( G \) - q G \) sigma^(- 1) \, wide
   G mapsto exp \( D - q \) G . $ <jets:target-transport>]

Here $D - q$ denotes the differential operator $f mapsto D \( f \) - q f$.
  An order-$k$ transition changes a target with constant term one first in degree $k$ of $G$,
  with contribution $- \( \( k - 4 \) b + h' \)$. Once lower coefficients are compatible,
  a leading degree-$k$ discrepancy is a cocycle in $L^k$.

The factor $t^2$ in (4.21) and the equality $B_0 = 0$ determine the order of the choices.
  For the field changes at step $k$, the first determinant equations they can affect are

$ upright("changed field coefficient") & upright("degree in ") A and B & upright("target coefficient")\
upright("global addition to ") A_(k - 1) & k & G_(k - 2)\
B_k & k & G_(k - 2)\
A_k & k + 1 & G_(k - 1) . $

The target coefficients in the last column remain fixed during these changes.
  In particular, we can still adjust $A_(k - 1)$ to solve the degree-$k$ equation without changing any determinant
  equation imposed at an earlier step. A new target coefficient $G_k$ will be chosen only after the fields match
  through index $k$; its field equation occurs in degree $k + 2$.

We will maintain the following invariant after step $n gt.eq 2$:
  the field rows through index $n$ match; the determinant equations (4.21) are imposed through degree $n$ in $A
  and B$; and the coefficients $G_0 = 1 \, G_1 \, dots.h \,
  G_n$ are chosen and compatible through degree $n$ under the transition maps.
  Thus target compatibility is known two determinant degrees beyond the equations already imposed:
  the equations involving $G_(n - 1)$ and $G_n$ are still to be solved.
  The preceding construction establishes this invariant for $n = 2$, with $G = 1$,
  since both transitions preserve $sigma$ exactly.

Suppose now that the invariant holds through step $k - 1$, where $k gt.eq 3$.
  The coefficient needed in the current determinant equation, $G_(k - 2)$, has already been chosen.
  On each chart the horizontal row must solve

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.23)")
 $ r and B_k = G_(k - 2) - sum_(a = 1)^(k - 1) A_a and B_(k - a) . $ <jets:horizontal-induction>]

This is an equation in $L^(k - 2)$. Indeed, under (4.5), the only additional terms in the transformation of the
  sum on the right are multiples of

$ sum_(a = 1)^(k - 1) B_a and B_(k - a) = 0 \; $

the terms cancel in pairs and any middle self-wedge vanishes.

===== 4.3.1 Making the right side divisible at $Z$. <making-the-right-side-divisible-at-z.>

We may add any row $a in H^0 \( C \, L^k \)^2$ to the previously chosen vertical coefficient $A_(k - 1)$.
  Differences of vertical lifts have this tensor type by (4.6).
  Such a change preserves matching through index $k - 1$: every previous nonsplit transition has positive order,
  so its action on the new term $t^k a E_t$ only changes indices at least $k$.
  It also preserves all determinant equations already imposed, because $B_0 = 0$;
  its first determinant contribution is $a and B_1$ in degree $k$.
  In particular, it need not preserve the as yet unimposed equation whose target is $G_(k - 2)$.

At each $q_j$ the row $B_1 \( q_j \) = R_j \( 0 \)$ is nonzero.
  The interpolation in Proposition 4(4) gives the surjection

$ H^0 \( C \, L^k \) arrow.r H^0 \( Z \, L^k \|_Z \) wide \( k gt.eq 3 \) . $

It permits arbitrary values of both entries of $a$ at all six points.
  The scalar maps $a \( q_j \) mapsto a \( q_j \) and B_1 \( q_j \)$ are surjective,
  so this freedom makes the right side of (4.23) vanish at every $q_j$.
  Since $r = z rho$ has only a simple common zero, no derivative condition is required.
  As at order two, division by $z$ on each disk and Steinness on $U_0$ now give holomorphic solutions $B_k$.

Although this global addition preserves all lower-order matching,
  the previous transitions can act on it in the degree currently being solved.
  The first transition contributes, at row index $k$,

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.24)")
 $ delta A_k = frac(k - 1, 3) m' a + m a' \, wide delta B_k = - m a . $ <jets:old-transition-effect>]

All other previous generators, and repeated brackets, affect later indices.
  The displayed horizontal contribution satisfies $r and \( - m a \) = a and \( m r \)$,
  exactly the corresponding determinant change on the disk.
  We include these terms in the horizontal and vertical comparisons below.

===== 4.3.2 Matching the horizontal rows. <matching-the-horizontal-rows.>

Choose any holomorphic vertical lifts of the newly selected $B_k$.
  After transport by the previous transitions, the field rows already agree at every lower index.
  The difference of their degree-$k$ determinants is therefore $r and delta B_k$, since $delta A_k and B_0 = 0$.
  Target compatibility through degree $k - 2$ in $G$ makes this difference zero.
  Thus the horizontal discrepancy is a holomorphic multiple of $r$ on each overlap,
  and (4.10) removes it by a choice of $h$ in an order-$k$ transition.
  Use $b = h'$ when $k = 3$, and $b = 0$ when $k gt.eq 4$. The new transition first affects $G$ in degree $k$,
  so cannot alter the target comparison just used in degree $k - 2$.

===== 4.3.3 Matching the vertical rows. <matching-the-vertical-rows.>

After horizontal matching, the remaining class is in $H^1 \( C \, L^(k + 1) \)^2$. For $k gt.eq 4$ it vanishes, since

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.25)")
 $ H^1 \( C \, L^(k + 1) \) tilde.eq H^0 \( C \, L^(3 - k) \)^(*) = 0 . $ <jets:vertical-vanishing>]

Holomorphic vertical corrections on the charts therefore solve the matching.
  They do not change the current determinant equation, because $B_0 = 0$.

At $k = 3$ the obstruction is $H^1 \( C \, K_C \)^2$, tested by the constants in the two entries.
  There is additional overlap freedom to kill it. On the $j$th overlap replace $h$ by $h +
  lambda_j \/ z$ and compensate on its disk by

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.26)")
 $ B_3 mapsto B_3 + frac(3 lambda_j r, z) . $ <jets:third-compensation>]

This is holomorphic and its wedge with $r$ is zero. It preserves both the current determinant equation and
  horizontal matching: the transition adds the opposite horizontal row $- 3 lambda_j r \/ z$.
  Keep the vertical lift $A_3$ unchanged in the chosen disk coordinate.
  This prescribes a holomorphic section of $cal(E)_3$ on that disk;
  in another split coordinate its vertical component acquires the term $a thin delta B_3$ dictated by (4.5).
  Changing a degree-three input row affects its transport under old positive-order transitions only at later
  indices. The change in the transported vertical difference at index three is therefore exactly

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.27)")
 $ lambda_j (r' / z + frac(2 r, z^2)) \, wide "Res"_0 [lambda_j (r' / z + frac(2 r, z^2)) d z] =
   3 lambda_j r' \( 0 \) . $ <jets:third-residue>]

The two entries here are coefficients of canonical differentials, since $L^4 = K_C$.
  The six rows $r' \( q_j \)$ span $bb(C)^2$: their projective directions are those of the pencil values
  $u \( q_j \)$, which are not all the same. Hence the map $\( lambda_1 \, dots.h \, lambda_6 \) mapsto sum_j 3
  lambda_j r' \( q_j \)$ has rank two and can cancel both obstruction entries.
  The resulting vertical class splits, completing order three.

The correction (4.26) can change the degree-four coefficient of $A and B$. This causes no conflict:
  $G_2 = 0$ has been selected, but its determinant equation is first imposed at step four.
  The degree-three equation already imposed is unchanged. Moreover the modified order-three transition still has
  $b = h'$ and so preserves $sigma$ exactly, leaving the transformation law of the chosen targets intact.

===== 4.3.4 Extending the determinant target. <extending-the-determinant-target.>

It remains to choose $G_k$ compatibly after the order-$k$ transition.
  For $k = 3$ all transitions preserve $sigma$, so we retain $G = 1$.
  At $k = 4$ the earlier transitions still preserve it exactly; thus the first target discrepancy is $-
  h'$ in degree four, by (4.22) with $b = 0$. The horizontal coefficient of an order-four generator has type
  $L^4 ⊗ cal(T)_C = cal(O)_C$. More explicitly, its split transformation is $tilde(h) = g^(4 - k) h$,
  which equals $h$ at $k = 4$. Consequently $h' d z = d h$ is an intrinsic exact differential on the overlap.
  Each such differential has zero contour residue. The sole obstruction to splitting this $L^4 =
  K_C$ cocycle is its pairing with the constant section of $cal(O)_C$, so it vanishes.
  There are holomorphic chart coefficients $G_4$ accounting for the discrepancy, with $G_1 = G_2 = G_3 = 0$.
  Transport by a previous positive-order modification changes a degree-four term only in degrees at least five.
  Equivalently, conjugating the order-four generator by the earlier transitions leaves its degree-four part
  unchanged. Thus the scalar transformation law of $h$, and the residue calculation using $d h$,
  apply intrinsically at this order.

For $k > 4$, once the lower target coefficients are compatible, the new leading discrepancy lies in $L^k$, and

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(4.28)")
 $ H^1 \( C \, L^k \) tilde.eq H^0 \( C \, L^(4 - k) \)^(*) = 0 . $ <jets:target-vanishing>]

Thus it always splits and defines $G_k$. In every case this target extension leaves all equations already imposed
  unchanged, since $G_k$ is used only in degree $k + 2$ of $A and B$. This completes the induction invariant.

We now choose where to stop. A row of index $k$ contributes degree $k +
  1$ in the logarithmic frame $\( E_t \, partial_z \)$, so matching through index seven prescribes a section
  modulo $cal(I)^9 cal(F)$. This is the threshold needed in Section 5:
  the successive quotients of the unprescribed tail are extensions of $L^(n - 4)$ by $L^n$,
  and both line bundles have vanishing first cohomology for $n gt.eq 9$.
  Lemma 13 turns these graded vanishings into holomorphic lifting on a realized neighborhood.

#thm([Proposition 4.3.])[
  (Finite compatible jets).  #emph[For the data of Propositions 4 and 7,
    there are finitely many holomorphic disk-to-outside transition germs,
    each a composition of flows (4.7) of orders $1 \, dots.h \, 7$, together with holomorphic local rows $A_k \,
    B_k$ for $0 lt.eq k lt.eq 7$, such that: the transitions fix $C$ and preserve its conormal bundle $L$;
    the vector fields (4.3) match through row index seven; and $A and B = t^2 \( 1 + O \( t \) \)$ on every chart.]
  #emph[On any surface neighborhood $Y$ realizing these transition germs, write $cal(I) =
    cal(O)_Y \( - C \)$ and $cal(F) = cal(T)_Y \( - log C \)$.
    The rows give two sections of $cal(F) \/ cal(I)^9 cal(F)$, lying in $cal(I) cal(F) \/ cal(I)^9 cal(F)$.
    Their determinant has leading term $t^4 E_t and partial_z$, equivalently $t^5 partial_t and partial_z$,
    with coefficient one.]
] <prop:jets>

#proof[
  Perform the first two steps and then the induction through $k = 7$.
    Only finitely many flows occur, and each is an actual holomorphic germ on an annular neighborhood of the zero
    section, as explained after Lemma 8. Terms with row indices $0 \, dots.h \,
    7$ have logarithmic-frame coefficients of degrees $1 \, dots.h \, 8$.
    Matching those terms is precisely matching modulo $cal(I)^9 cal(F)$.
    Finally (4.4) and the degree-two determinant equation give
  $ v_1 and v_2 = t^4 \( 1 + O \( t \) \) E_t and partial_z = t^5 \( 1 + O \( t \) \) partial_t and partial_z . $
  This statement uses only finite compatible jets; analytic realization and the lifting of these quotient sections
    are addressed next. ◻
]

== 5 Analytic realization and contraction <analytic:section>

We now realize the finite gluing data of Proposition 10 by an actual complex surface.
  The two prescribed vector fields will then be lifted from their finite jets by a cohomology vanishing argument.
  The realization uses only the finite list of genuine holomorphic flow maps already constructed.
  The formal-functions theorem used afterwards computes a cohomology group on that realized neighborhood.
  Throughout this section, powers of an ideal denote ordinary ideal powers,
  and cohomology is analytic sheaf cohomology. We use coherence of analytic structure and ideal sheaves as in [7]
  Chapter II, Theorems 3.19 and 4.29.

=== 5.1 A Hausdorff neighborhood from the finite overlap maps <a-hausdorff-neighborhood-from-the-finite-overlap-maps>

#thm([Lemma 5.1.])[
  #emph[The overlap maps of Proposition 10 define, after shrinking their domains,
    a smooth Hausdorff complex surface neighborhood $Y$ of $C$. Its conormal bundle is $L$, and hence
$ C^2 = - 10 . $

 Writing $cal(I) = cal(O)_Y \( - C \)$ and $cal(F) = cal(T)_Y \( - log C \)$,
   the prescribed rows define two global sections
$ macron(v)_1 \, macron(v)_2 in H^0 \( Y \, cal(F) \/ cal(I)^9 cal(F) \) . $

 In divisor coordinates $\( t \, z \)$, their local representatives have logarithmic determinant
   $t^4 \( 1 + O \( t \) \)$ and ordinary determinant $t^5 \( 1 + O \( t \) \)$.]
] <analytic:realization>

#proof[
  Let $N = L^(- 1)$ be the split normal bundle. All changes to its disk-to-outside identifications are finite
    compositions of flows of holomorphic vector fields
  $ D = t^k #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] b \( z \) t partial_t +
    h \( z \) partial_z #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)] \, wide k gt.eq 1 \, $
  on punctured disk neighborhoods. On a compact annulus in any such overlap,
    these vector fields vanish along the zero section. The local existence theorem for holomorphic differential
    equations, applied uniformly on that compact annulus, therefore gives the required time-one maps and their
    inverses after shrinking the fiber radius. There are only finitely many flows.
    Shrinking successively gives their compositions on neighborhoods of the same compact annular bands.
    Denote the resulting inside-to-outside maps by $Phi_j$, $1 lt.eq j lt.eq 6$.
  Here is a quotient construction that also verifies the separation property.
    Choose mutually disjoint coordinate disks $D_j$ about the six points, and in each choose radii
  $ 0 < a_j < a'_j < b'_j < b_j $
  so that $Phi_j$ and its inverse are defined on neighborhoods of the closed annulus $a_j lt.eq \| z_j \| lt.eq
    b_j$ in their respective split bundles. Their domains may be taken somewhat wider than these closed annuli.
    Retain a small open neighborhood $V_j$ of the zero section over $\| z_j \| < b'_j$,
    and a small open neighborhood $V_0$ in $N$ over
  $ C \\ union.big_(j = 1)^6 { \| z_j \| lt.eq a'_j } . $
  Choose the transverse thicknesses small enough that the maps are defined throughout the retained parts of the
    closed annuli and
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.1)")
   $ \| z_j \( Phi_j \( p \) \) - z_j \( p \) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|] < frac(a'_j - a_j, 2)
     $ <analytic:collar-bound>]
  there. This is possible because each map restricts to the identity on the zero section.
    We also make the images of the retained annuli lie over their respective disjoint disks $D_j$.
  Set
  $ A_j = { p in V_j : a_j < \| z_j \( p \) \| < b_j \, med Phi_j \( p \) in V_0 } \, $
  and identify $p in A_j$ with $Phi_j \( p \) in V_0$. These are biholomorphic identifications between open
    subsets. A point of $V_0$ is in at most one of their images, since those images lie over disjoint disks.
    Each equivalence class in $V_0 union.sq V_1 union.sq dots.h.c union.sq V_6$ thus has at most two elements.
    In particular the equivalence relation consists exactly of the diagonal, these six graphs, and their inverses;
    no further identifications or triple-overlap equations occur.
  Each graph is closed relative to $V_j times V_0$. Indeed, suppose $p_nu in A_j$, $p_nu arrow.r p in V_j$,
    and $Phi_j \( p_nu \) arrow.r q in V_0$. The source base radii have limit at least $a_j$ and less than $b'_j <
    b_j$. The flow is defined across the closed annulus, so continuity gives $q = Phi_j \( p \)$.
    If the limiting source radius were $a_j$, inequality (5.1) would give $\| z_j \( q \) \| < a'_j$,
    contrary to $q in V_0$. Thus $a_j < \| z_j \( p \) \| < b_j$ and $p in A_j$, as required.
    Possible fiber boundaries cause no difficulty because both limiting points are assumed to belong to the
    retained open pieces.
  The equivalence relation is therefore closed. The quotient map is open,
    since it glues open sets by biholomorphisms. An open quotient map with closed equivalence relation has
    Hausdorff target: two inequivalent representatives have product neighborhoods disjoint from the relation,
    and the images of these neighborhoods are disjoint open sets.
    Moreover, the quotient map restricts on every $V_i$ to an injective open map.
    The complex structures on these pieces consequently give the quotient a smooth complex surface structure.
    The images of their zero sections give the original compact curve $C$ with its original complex structure.
  Every modifying flow fixes $C$ and induces the identity on its conormal bundle.
    In the split identification its coordinate expansion has $t' = t + O \( t^2 \)$,
    although its base coordinate may change by $O \( t \)$. Composing with the original split changes therefore
    leaves $cal(I) \/ cal(I)^2 tilde.eq L$. This proves $C^2 = - deg L = - 10$.
  In the logarithmic basis $\( t partial_t \, partial_z \)$ the prescribed fields have coefficients $t A_i \,
    t B_i$. Rows with indices $0$ through $7$ prescribe these coefficients through degree $8$ in $t$.
    The matching in Proposition 10 is therefore precisely matching modulo $cal(I)^9
    cal(F)$ on the actual overlap maps. It gives the claimed quotient sections. Their logarithmic determinant is
  $ t^2 \( A and B \) = t^4 \( 1 + O \( t \) \) . $
  Replacing $t partial_t$ by $partial_t$ multiplies this determinant by $t$, giving the stated ordinary determinant. ◻
]

=== 5.2 Vanishing and holomorphic lifting <vanishing-and-holomorphic-lifting>

The finite jets now live on a Hausdorff surface. To lift them to holomorphic fields,
  we prove that the first cohomology of $cal(I)^9 cal(F)$ vanishes after shrinking about $C$.
  The logarithmic tangent sheaf $cal(F)$ is locally free of rank two. Restriction to $C$ gives the exact sequence

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.2)")
 $ 0 arrow.r cal(O)_C arrow.r cal(F) \|_C arrow.r cal(T)_C arrow.r 0 . $ <analytic:log-sequence>]

Here the first map sends $1$ to the class of the normal Euler field $t partial_t$,
  and the second is restriction of a tangent vector field to $C$.
  The Euler class is unchanged when the local equation of $C$ is multiplied by a unit.
  The sequence is thus intrinsic; a splitting is unnecessary.

#thm([Lemma 5.2.])[
  #emph[For every $n gt.eq 9$,
$ H^1 \( C \, cal(F) \|_C ⊗ L^n \) = 0 . $

 Consequently, for $cal(E) = cal(I)^9 cal(F)$ and every $r gt.eq 1$,
$ H^1 \( Y \, cal(E) \/ cal(I)^r cal(E) \) = 0 . $

]
] <analytic:graded-vanishing>

#proof[
  Since $K_C tilde.eq L^4$, tensoring (5.2) by $L^n$ gives
  $ 0 arrow.r L^n arrow.r cal(F) \|_C ⊗ L^n arrow.r L^(n - 4) arrow.r 0 . $
  Serre duality identifies the duals of the first cohomology groups of the outer terms with
  $ H^0 \( C \, L^(4 - n) \) quad upright("and") quad H^0 \( C \, L^(8 - n) \) \, $
  respectively. Both vanish for $n gt.eq 9$, as $deg L > 0$. The first assertion follows from the cohomology sequence.
  Because $C$ is a Cartier divisor and $cal(F)$ is locally free,
  $ cal(I)^n cal(F) \/ cal(I)^(n + 1) cal(F) tilde.eq cal(F) \|_C ⊗ L^n . $
  The quotient $cal(E) \/ cal(I)^r cal(E) = cal(I)^9 cal(F) \/ cal(I)^(9 + r)
    cal(F)$ has a finite filtration with precisely these graded terms for $9 lt.eq n < 9 + r$.
    Their first cohomology vanishes by the first assertion. Successive cohomology sequences give the second
    assertion; the quotient is supported on the compact curve $C$. ◻
]

#thm([Lemma 5.3.])[
  #emph[After shrinking $Y$ about $C$, the sections $macron(v)_1 \,
    macron(v)_2$ lift to holomorphic sections $v_1 \, v_2$ of $cal(F)$. They form a frame for $cal(T)_(Y \\ C)$.]
] <analytic:actual-lifting>

#proof[
  The intersection matrix of $C$ is the negative definite matrix $\( - 10 \)$.
    Grauert’s contraction criterion therefore contracts $C$ to a point $x$ of a normal complex surface:
  $ pi : Y arrow.r X \, wide pi^(- 1) \( x \) = C \, wide Y \\ C tilde.eq X \\ { x } \, $
  where the fiber equality is set-theoretic, and $pi_(*) cal(O)_Y = cal(O)_X$.
    We use the criterion in [14] §4.8(e), pp. 366–367. The contraction is proper and is a biholomorphism off the
    exceptional curve. It is the holomorphic reduction in that theorem,
    whose target is normal when the source is normal; these properties are part of the reduction and contraction
    statements in [14] §2, p. 337 and Definition 3, pp. 339–340.
    We choose a sufficiently small Stein representative $X$ about $x$ and replace $Y$ by its inverse image.
    In particular $X \\ { x }$ is smooth.
  We prove $R^1 pi_(*) cal(E) = 0$ for $cal(E) = cal(I)^9 cal(F)$, keeping track of the two ideal filtrations.
    Let $frak(m)_x$ be the ideal sheaf of $x$ in $X$ and put $cal(J) = frak(m)_x cal(O)_Y$.
    Both $cal(J)$ and $cal(I)$ vanish exactly on $C$, and $cal(I)$ is radical.
    The analytic Nullstellensatz [7] Chapter II, Theorem 4.22 and Noetherianity give an inclusion $cal(I)^a
    subset.eq cal(J)$ locally at each point of $C$ for some positive integer $a$.
    A finite cover of the compact curve gives a single exponent. After shrinking the representative,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.3)")
   $ cal(I)^a subset.eq cal(J) subset.eq cal(I) \, wide cal(I)^(a r) subset.eq cal(J)^r subset.eq cal(I)^r
     quad \( r gt.eq 1 \) . $ <analytic:cofinal>]
  Such a shrinking may be made on $X$: for a proper map, every open neighborhood of its compact fiber contains the
    inverse image of a sufficiently small neighborhood of the image point.
  The proper analytic direct-image theorem makes $R^1 pi_(*) cal(E)$ coherent.
    The analytic theorem on formal functions identifies its completed stalk with
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.4)")
   $ accent(\( R^1 pi_(*) cal(E) \)_x, ̂) tilde.eq "proj lim"_(r gt.eq 1)
     H^1 \( Y \, cal(E) \/ cal(J)^r cal(E) \) . $ <analytic:formal-functions>]
  We use [13] §6, Hauptsätze I and IIa, p. 59: the hypotheses are a proper holomorphic map and a coherent analytic
    sheaf. In particular no flatness of $pi$ is required. The quotients in (5.4) are supported on the
    infinitesimal fibers over $x$, so their cohomology is independent of a further shrinking of the representative.
  For clarity, the right side vanishes by an explicit factorization,
    without identifying the individual cohomology groups for the two filtrations. The inclusions in (5.3) give
  $ cal(J)^(a r) cal(E) subset.eq cal(I)^(a r) cal(E) subset.eq cal(J)^r cal(E) . $
  Hence the transition map from index $a r$ to index $r$ in (5.4) factors through
  $ H^1 \( Y \, cal(E) \/ cal(I)^(a r) cal(E) \) = 0 $
  by Lemma 12. The inverse system is therefore pro-zero, and its inverse limit is zero.
    Since the stalk $\( R^1 pi_(*) cal(E) \)_x$ is a finite module over the Noetherian local ring
    $cal(O)_(X \, x)$, its zero completion implies that the stalk itself is zero, by Nakayama’s lemma.
    Away from $x$ the map $pi$ is an isomorphism, so the same higher direct image vanishes there.
    We obtain $R^1 pi_(*) cal(E) = 0$.
  The sheaf $pi_(*) cal(E)$ is also coherent. Cartan’s Theorem B on the Stein space $X$,
    together with the Leray spectral sequence for $pi$, now gives
  $ H^1 \( Y \, cal(E) \) = 0 . $
  The cohomology sequence of
  $ 0 arrow.r cal(E) arrow.r cal(F) arrow.r cal(F) \/ cal(I)^9 cal(F) arrow.r 0 $
  therefore lifts both $macron(v)_i$ to actual holomorphic sections $v_i$ of $cal(F)$.
  It remains to check that arbitrary such lifts retain the required frame.
    In each divisor chart, let $v_i^(\( 8 \))$ be the polynomial representative of the prescribed jet.
    It belongs to $cal(I) cal(F)$, and
  $ v_i - v_i^(\( 8 \)) in cal(I)^9 cal(F) . $
  Thus the change in their wedge belongs to $cal(I)^10 det cal(F)$.
    By Lemma 11, the logarithmic determinant of the lifted pair is still $t^4 \( 1 + O \( t \) \)$;
    its leading coefficient is $1$ even at the common zeros of the leading row $r$. Its ordinary determinant is
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.5)")
   $ v_1 and v_2 = t^5 u \( t \, z \) thin partial_t and partial_z \, wide u \( 0 \, z \) =
     1 . $ <analytic:ordinary-determinant>]
  The unit condition holds on a neighborhood of each point of $C$.
    Compactness gives one neighborhood on which all these local factors are nonvanishing.
    Properness allows a further shrinking of $X$ whose inverse image lies in this neighborhood.
    Equation (5.5) then proves that $v_1 \, v_2$ frame $cal(T)_(Y \\ C)$. ◻
]

=== 5.3 The singular point and its derivations <the-singular-point-and-its-derivations>

#thm([Proposition 5.4.])[
  #emph[There is a normal complex surface germ $\( X \, x \)$ with an isolated singular point and free analytic
    derivation sheaf of rank two:
$ cal(T)_X = cal(H) o m_(cal(O)_X) \( Omega_X^1 \, cal(O)_X \) tilde.eq cal(O)_X^(xor 2) . $

 It has a resolution $pi : Y arrow.r X$ whose exceptional set is the smooth curve $C$ of genus $21$ and
   self-intersection $- 10$.]
] <prop:analytic-counterexample>

#proof[
  Take the contraction constructed in Lemma 13. Suppose first that $x$ were a smooth point.
    A sufficiently small neighborhood of $x$ would admit a nowhere-vanishing holomorphic two-form $omega$.
    Its pullback would be holomorphic on $Y$ and nonvanishing off $C$. Thus
  $ "div" \( pi^(*) omega \) = a C wide \( a gt.eq 0 \) \, quad upright("and hence") quad K_Y dot.op C =
    a C^2 lt.eq 0 . $
  Adjunction, however, gives
  $ K_Y dot.op C = 2 g \( C \) - 2 - C^2 = 40 + 10 = 50 \, $
  a contradiction. The point $x$ is singular, and it is the only singular point after the chosen shrinking.
  Write $X^compose = X \\ { x }$ and $j : X^compose arrow.r.hook X$.
    The two fields of Lemma 13 descend to a holomorphic frame $w_1 \, w_2$ on $X^compose$.
    Normality in dimension two gives the Hartogs extension property [7] Chapter II, Corollary 7.8
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(5.6)")
   $ cal(O)_X tilde.eq j_(*) cal(O)_(X^compose) . $ <analytic:hartogs>]
  This property extends the fields as derivations, as follows.
    Embed a representative of $\( X \, x \)$ in a smooth analytic germ with coordinate functions $x_1 \, dots.h \,
    x_e$. For each $i$ and $alpha$, the holomorphic function $w_i \( x_alpha \)$ on $X^compose$ extends uniquely
    to a function $a_(i alpha)$ on $X$. If $f$ is any local defining equation of the embedding, then
  $ sum_(alpha = 1)^e a_(i alpha) frac(partial f, partial x_alpha) = 0 $
  on $X^compose$ and hence on $X$, by density and reducedness.
    The tuple $\( a_(i alpha) \)_alpha$ consequently defines a section of $cal(H) o
    m_(cal(O)_X) \( Omega_X^1 \, cal(O)_X \)$ extending $w_i$. We continue to denote that extension by $w_i$.
  For any local analytic derivation $w$ on $X$, the frame on $X^compose$ gives unique holomorphic coefficients
    $h_1 \, h_2$ there such that $w = h_1 w_1 + h_2 w_2$. Both coefficients extend by (5.6).
    The extended equality holds on $X$, since applying its difference to the embedding coordinates gives
    holomorphic functions vanishing on the dense open set $X^compose$. This proves surjectivity of the map
  $ cal(O)_X^(xor 2) arrow.r cal(T)_X \, wide \( h_1 \, h_2 \) mapsto h_1 w_1 + h_2 w_2 . $
  If a pair lies in its kernel, both coefficients vanish on $X^compose$ because $w_1 \, w_2$ are a frame there,
    and therefore vanish on $X$. The map is also injective, proving the assertion. ◻
]

#prodef([Remark 5.5.])[
  The construction also determines its canonical discrepancy and a lower bound for its geometric genus.
    The two-form $omega^compose$ dual to the frame $w_1 \, w_2$ generates the canonical sheaf on $X^compose$.
    Extending reflexively across the normal surface point gives $omega_X tilde.eq cal(O)_X$; equivalently,
    we may choose the canonical divisor $K_X = 0$. Since a normal surface is Cohen–Macaulay,
    this reflexive canonical sheaf is its dualizing sheaf, so $X$ is also Gorenstein;
    this surface criterion is recalled in [9] §2.4, Definition 2.18 and the following paragraph.
    By (5.5), its pullback is
  $ pi^(*) omega^compose = t^(- 5) u \( t \, z \)^(- 1) thin d t and d z . $
  It is meromorphic on $Y$ with divisor exactly $- 5 C$. Thus
  $ K_Y = pi^(*) K_X - 5 C \, $
  so the discrepancy of $C$ is $- 5$.
  For the geometric genus, put $cal(O)_(n C) = cal(O)_Y \/ cal(I)^n$.
    Riemann–Roch and the established equality $h^0 \( C \, L \) = 2$ give
  $ h^1 \( C \, L \) = h^0 \( C \, L^3 \) = 12 . $
  In the sequence
  $ 0 arrow.r L arrow.r cal(O)_(2 C) arrow.r cal(O)_C arrow.r 0 \, $
  the constant section of $cal(O)_C$ lifts to $cal(O)_(2 C)$.
    Since $H^2 \( C \, L \) = 0$, its cohomology sequence gives
  $ h^1 \( cal(O)_(2 C) \) = h^1 \( C \, L \) + h^1 \( C \, cal(O)_C \) = 12 + 21 = 33 . $
  For every $n gt.eq 1$, the sequence with kernel $L^n$ similarly shows that $H^1 \( cal(O)_(\( n + 1 \) C) \)
    arrow.r H^1 \( cal(O)_(n C) \)$ is surjective. The theorem on formal functions,
    with the cofinal filtrations in (5.3), identifies their inverse limit with the completion of
    $\( R^1 pi_(*) cal(O)_Y \)_x$. This stalk has finite length,
    since the coherent higher direct image is supported at $x$, and thus equals its completion.
    The inverse limit maps onto $H^1 \( cal(O)_(2 C) \)$, proving
  $ p_g \( X \, x \) = dim_(bb(C)) \( R^1 pi_(*) cal(O)_Y \)_x gt.eq 33 . $
  The exceptional curve has genus $g = 21$ and its dual graph has first Betti number $b = 0$.
    Consequently $p_g - g - b gt.eq 12$.
] <analytic:invariants>

== 6 Algebraization and the affine counterexample <algebra:section>

We now pass from the analytic germ to an affine variety. Approximating the defining equations does not by itself
  retain their relations. We approximate the equations and a finite relation matrix together;
  their exact zero product, together with sufficiently close approximation,
  will recover the same complete local ring. Tangent freeness then descends from the continuous derivations of
  that ring. This follows the equation-and-relation determinacy method of Hironaka [20] §3, Theorem 3.3,
  used in Artin’s isolated-singularity algebraization [1] Theorems 3.8–3.9.
  We prove the finite-determinacy statement needed here, including fixed estimates that avoid a
  complete-intersection hypothesis.

=== 6.1 Finite determinacy of equations with relations <finite-determinacy-of-equations-with-relations>

Put

$ P = bb(C) \[ \[ x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e \] \] \, wide frak(n) = \( x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e \) \, wide
  I = \( f_1 \, dots.h \, f_s \) \, wide R = P \/ I \, $

where $f = \( f_1 \, dots.h \, f_s \) in frak(n) P^s$ is a row.
  Write $frak(m)_R = frak(n) R$, and let an overbar denote reduction modulo $I$.
  Choose an $s times t$ matrix $D$ whose columns generate all the relations on $f$. Thus

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.1)")
 $ P^t arrow.r^D P^s arrow.r^f I arrow.r 0 $ <algebra:ideal-presentation>]

is exact. Define

$ psi : R^s arrow.r R^t \, wide v mapsto v macron(D) \, wide K = ker psi \, wide J =
  sum_(j = 1)^e R thin overline(partial_j f) . $

Differentiating $f D = 0$ and reducing modulo $I$ shows that $J subset K$.
  Right-exact reduction of (6.1) gives a presentation of $I \/ I^2$; its dual therefore identifies

#[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.2)")
 $ K = "Hom"_R \( I \/ I^2 \, R \) \, wide T^1 = K \/ J . $ <algebra:conormal-dual>]

In particular, no exactness on the left after reduction has been assumed.

#thm([Lemma 6.1.])[
  (Finite determinacy with a relation matrix).  #emph[Suppose that $T^1$ in (6.2) has finite length.
    There exist integers $N \, n_0$ depending only on $f \, D$ such that the following holds.
    If a row $g in P^s$ and an $s times t$ matrix $B$ satisfy
$ g B = 0 \, wide g - f in frak(n)^(n_0) P^s \, wide B - D in frak(n)^N P^(s times t) \, $

 then there are a continuous $bb(C)$-algebra automorphism $Phi$ of $P$ and an invertible matrix $U in
   "GL"_s \( P \)$ such that
$ f = Phi \( g \) U . $

 One can take $Phi \( x_j \) equiv x_j med \( mod med frak(n)^2 \)$ and $U equiv 1 med \( mod med frak(n) \)$.
   Consequently $P \/ \( g \) tilde.eq P \/ \( f \)$ as complete local $bb(C)$-algebras.]
] <algebra:finite-determinacy>

#proof[
  We improve the equations one order at a time. The exact relation $g B = 0$ makes the equation error,
    reduced modulo $I$, nearly belong to $K$. Artin–Rees lets us correct it into $K$;
    the finite length of $K \/ J$ then expresses the sufficiently high-order corrected class by coordinate
    derivatives. A change of generators removes the remaining $I$-valued error.
    We arrange these corrections so that the equation precision increases at every step while the relation
    precision stays fixed. The resulting coordinate and generator changes converge in $P$.
  We first choose the fixed bounds. For a map $u : M arrow.r
    M'$ of finite modules over a Noetherian local ring with maximal ideal $frak(a)$,
    the Artin–Rees lemma [28] Tag 00IN, applied to $"im" u subset M'$ gives a constant $a gt.eq 0$ such that
  $ "im" u ∩ frak(a)^r M' subset u \( frak(a)^(r - a) M \) wide \( r gt.eq a \) . $
  Choose nonnegative constants $alpha \, beta \, d$ so that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.3)")
   $ "im" psi ∩ frak(m)_R^r R^t & subset psi \( frak(m)_R^(r - alpha) R^s \) \,\
K ∩ frak(m)_R^r R^s & subset frak(m)_R^(r - beta) K \,\
I^(xor s) ∩ frak(n)^r P^s & subset { f C : C in frak(n)^(r - d) P^(s times s) } $ <algebra:ar-image>]
  whenever the corresponding exponents are nonnegative. In (6.3),
    Artin–Rees is applied to the map $P^(s times s) arrow.r P^s$, $C mapsto f C$, with image $I^(xor s)$;
    it is the filtration induced from $P^s$ that is relevant.
  Choose $ell gt.eq 0$ with $frak(m)_R^ell K subset J$ and set
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.4)")
   $ c_0 = beta + ell \, wide c = c_0 + d \, wide N gt.eq alpha + 1 \, wide n_0 gt.eq max { c + N \, c + 2 \,
     2 c + 1 } . $ <algebra:constants>]
  We first describe one improvement step. Suppose $n gt.eq n_0$ and
  $ h = g - f in frak(n)^n P^s \, wide B - D in frak(n)^N P^(s times t) \, wide g B = 0 . $
  The exact relation, reduced modulo the fixed ideal $I$, gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.5)")
   $ macron(h) macron(D) = macron(g) macron(D) = - macron(g) \( macron(B) - macron(D) \) = -
     macron(h) \( macron(B) - macron(D) \) in frak(m)_R^(n + N) R^t . $ <algebra:high-order-relation>]
  The factor $macron(h)$ on the right is what provides order $n + N$. By (6.3), there is
  $ macron(epsilon.alt) in frak(m)_R^(n + N - alpha) R^s subset frak(m)_R^(n + 1) R^s \, wide
    psi \( macron(epsilon.alt) \) = psi \( macron(h) \) . $
  Hence
  $ kappa = macron(h) - macron(epsilon.alt) in K ∩ frak(m)_R^n R^s subset frak(m)_R^(n - beta) K subset
    frak(m)_R^(n - c_0) J . $
  Here the last inclusion uses $frak(m)_R^ell K subset J$, with the remaining factor of order $n - beta - ell =
    n - c_0$. Lift coefficients expressing $kappa$ in the displayed generators of $J$ to elements $a_j in
    frak(n)^(n - c_0)$, and lift $macron(epsilon.alt)$ to $epsilon.alt in frak(n)^(n + 1) P^s$. Then
  $ w = h - sum_(j = 1)^e a_j partial_j f - epsilon.alt in I^(xor s) ∩ frak(n)^(n - c_0) P^s . $
  The order of this ideal-valued remainder need only be $n - c_0$,
    since the lifted derivative combination may have lower order than $h$:
    a derivative of a defining equation can be a unit. Using (6.3), write $w =
    f C$ with $C in frak(n)^(n - c) P^(s times s)$. We have proved
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.6)")
   $ h = sum_(j = 1)^e a_j partial_j f + f C + epsilon.alt \, wide a_j \, C in frak(n)^(n - c) \, wide
     epsilon.alt in frak(n)^(n + 1) P^s . $ <algebra:linear-correction>]
  Thus the loss $d$ for lifting the ideal-valued remainder has been included in the fixed constant $c$.
  Put $q = n - c$, and define
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.7)")
   $ phi.alt \( x_j \) = x_j - a_j \, wide M = \( 1 + C \)^(- 1) \, wide g_(upright("new")) = phi.alt \( g \) M \,
     wide B_(upright("new")) = \( 1 + C \) phi.alt \( B \) . $ <algebra:one-step>]
  Our bounds ensure
  $ q gt.eq max { N \, 2 } \, wide 2 q gt.eq n + 1 \, wide n + q - 1 gt.eq n + 1 . $
  In particular, $phi.alt$ is a formal automorphism with identity linear part, and $M$ is invertible.
    Formal Taylor expansion gives
  $ phi.alt \( f \) - f + sum_j a_j partial_j f in frak(n)^(2 q) P^s \, wide phi.alt \( h \) -
    h in frak(n)^(n + q - 1) P^s . $
  Combining these estimates with (6.6) gives
  $ phi.alt \( g \) equiv f \( 1 + C \) med \( mod med frak(n)^(n + 1) P^s \) \, wide g_(upright("new")) -
    f in frak(n)^(n + 1) P^s . $
  The relations in (6.7) transform exactly:
  $ g_(upright("new")) B_(upright("new")) = phi.alt \( g \) \( 1 + C \)^(- 1) \( 1 + C \) phi.alt \( B \) = 0 . $
  Moreover, substitution by $phi.alt$ changes any power series by an element of $frak(n)^q$,
    and $C$ has order at least $q$. Therefore
  $ B_(upright("new")) - D = C phi.alt \( B \) + \( phi.alt \( B \) - B \) + \( B - D \) in frak(n)^N P^(s times t) . $
  The fixed relation precision $N$ is preserved, while the equation precision increases from $n$ to $n + 1$.
  Starting with $\( g_(n_0) \, B_(n_0) \) = \( g \, B \)$, repeat this step for $n = n_0 \, n_0 + 1 \, dots.h$.
    Write $phi.alt_n \, M_n$ for its corrections. For completeness, the cumulative coordinate and generator changes obey
  $ Phi_(n_0) = "id" \, quad U_(n_0) = 1 \, wide Phi_(n + 1) = phi.alt_n compose Phi_n \, wide U_(n + 1) =
    phi.alt_n \( U_n \) M_n \, $
  so that $g_n = Phi_n \( g \) U_n$ at every stage. Since
  $ \( phi.alt_n - "id" \) \( P \) subset frak(n)^(n - c) \, wide M_n - 1 in frak(n)^(n - c) P^(s times s) \, $
  the consecutive images $Phi_n \( x_j \)$ and the consecutive matrices $U_n$ differ in orders tending to
    infinity. They converge in the complete ring $P$ to $Phi \( x_j \)$ and $U$.
    The resulting substitution $Phi$ has identity linear part and hence is an automorphism, while $U equiv
    1 med \( mod med frak(n) \)$ is invertible. Taking limits in $g_n = Phi_n \( g \) U_n$ yields $f =
    Phi \( g \) U$, as required. ◻
]

The original matrix $D$ must generate all the relations, in order that (6.2) hold.
  No such assertion is needed for the approximating matrix $B$:
  its exact zero product and its closeness to $D$ are sufficient.
  Thus Lemma 16 concerns solutions of the finite system $g B = 0$,
  not arbitrary truncations of a list of defining equations.

=== 6.2 The algebraic neighborhood <the-algebraic-neighborhood>

#thm([Proposition 6.2.])[
  (Algebraization of an isolated analytic germ).  #emph[Let $\( X \, x \)$ be a normal complex analytic germ of
    dimension $d gt.eq 1$, smooth away from $x$, and suppose that its holomorphic tangent module is free of rank
    $d$. There exist a finitely generated normal integral $bb(C)$-algebra $A$ and a maximal ideal $frak(p) subset
    A$ such that
$ dim A = d \, wide A \/ frak(p) = bb(C) \, wide hat(A_(frak(p))) tilde.eq hat(cal(O)_(X \, x)) \, wide
  "Der"_(bb(C)) \( A \) tilde.eq A^d . $

 The affine variety $"Spec" A$ can be chosen smooth away from $frak(p)$.
   If $x$ is singular, $A_(frak(p))$ is nonregular.]
] <prop:algebraization>

#proof[
  #emph[The completed analytic presentation.] Embed the germ in a smooth analytic germ and write
  $ S = cal(O)_(X \, x) = bb(C) { x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e } \/ I_(upright("an")) . $
  Choose a finite row $f$ generating $I_(upright("an"))$ and a finite matrix $D$ generating all its relations over
    the convergent power-series ring. Completion is exact on finite modules,
    so they give the presentation (6.1) over $P = bb(C) \[ \[ x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e \] \]$, with $R = hat(S) =
    P \/ \( f \)$. Complex analytic local rings are excellent [6] Proposition 2.1(1);
    thus the completion of the normal local ring $S$ is a normal local domain. Also
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.8)")
   $ dim R = dim S = d \, wide R upright(" is regular if and only if ") S upright(" is regular") .
     $ <algebra:completion-invariants>]
  We use here the usual exactness and permanence properties of completion [28].
  The convergent version of (6.2) is the conormal dual of the analytic embedding.
    On the smooth locus the tangent-to-normal map is surjective:
    a smooth analytic subspace of a complex manifold is locally a regular embedding.
    Its cokernel is consequently a coherent module supported at the isolated point $x$, and has finite length.
    Flat completion identifies this cokernel with the module $T^1 = K \/ J$ above.
    Lemma 16 therefore applies to $f \, D$.
  We also record precisely which differentials are being completed.
    The module of holomorphic differentials of $S$ has the finite Jacobian presentation with generators $d
    x_j$ and relations $d f_i$. Its dual is the holomorphic tangent module. Flat completion of this kernel yields
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.9)")
   $ "Der"_(bb(C))^(upright("cont")) \( R \, R \) =
     ker (R^e arrow.r R^s \, \( v_j \) mapsto sum_(j = 1)^e v_j overline(partial_j f)) tilde.eq R^d .
     $ <algebra:continuous-tangent>]
  Here “continuous” refers to the maximal-ideal topology. A continuous derivation of a power-series quotient is
    determined by its values on the $x_j$, and the displayed relations are precisely the conditions for it to
    descend from $P$. All completed differential modules used below are these finite continuous differential modules.
  #emph[Approximation of equations and relations.] Let
  $ H = #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\(] bb(C) \[ x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e \]_(\( x_1 \, dots.h \, x_e \))
    #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\)]^h $
  be the henselization, whose completion is $P$. This is precisely a henselization of a finite-type algebra over
    the field $bb(C)$ at a prime, as covered by Artin’s Theorem 1.10.
    Apply that approximation theorem over $H$ to the polynomial system in the $s +
    s t$ entries of a row $G$ and a matrix $B$,
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.10)")
   $ sum_(i = 1)^s G_i B_(i nu) = 0 wide \( 1 lt.eq nu lt.eq t \) \, $ <algebra:artin-system>]
  with formal solution $\( G \, B \) = \( f \, D \)$. It gives a row $g$ and a matrix $B$ with entries in $H$,
    satisfying $g B = 0$ exactly and agreeing with $f \, D$ to any prescribed finite order [1] Theorem 1.10;
    see also [29]. Choose that order at least $max { n_0 \, N }$, with $n_0 \, N$ from Lemma 16. The lemma gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.11)")
   $ P \/ \( g \) tilde.eq R . $ <algebra:formal-equivalence>]
  The henselization is the filtered colimit of pointed étale neighborhoods of the origin,
    with the same residue field [30]. Thus the finitely many entries of $g \,
    B$ are represented on a single such neighborhood. After passing to a later neighborhood,
    the finitely many identities (6.10) hold there as well. If this stage is expressed over the local polynomial
    ring, finite presentation and clearing finitely many denominators spread it to a neighborhood of the origin in
    affine space; shrinking preserves the marked point and makes the morphism étale.
    After an affine shrinking the identities hold in its coordinate ring.
    We obtain an affine étale neighborhood $"Spec" E arrow.r bb(A)_(bb(C))^e$ with a marked point $frak(q)$ over
    the origin, residue field $bb(C)$, and a row $g$ of elements of $E$ vanishing at $frak(q)$.
    Its completed ambient local ring is $P$. Put
  $ A' = E \/ \( g \) \, wide frak(p)' = frak(q) \/ \( g \) \, wide T = A'_(frak(p)') . $
  Then $A'$ is of finite type over $bb(C)$, and exactness of completion together with (6.11) gives
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.12)")
   $ hat(T) = P \/ \( g \) tilde.eq R . $ <algebra:completed-local-ring>]
  In particular, this construction preserves the complete local ring, including its dimension and its normality.
  #emph[Descent of tangent freeness and local ring properties.] Because the ambient neighborhood is étale over
    $bb(A)_(bb(C))^e$, its differentials have basis $d x_1 \, dots.h \, d x_e$ near the marked point.
    Completing the finite presentation of $Omega_(T \/ bb(C))^1$ therefore gives the continuous Jacobian
    presentation of $P \/ \( g \)$. For a finitely presented module, flat base change commutes with its dual:
    this follows by expressing the dual as the kernel of a map between finite free modules.
    The continuous chain rule under (6.12) and (6.9) consequently give
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.13)")
   $ "Der"_(bb(C)) \( T \) ⊗_T hat(T) tilde.eq "Der"_(bb(C))^(upright("cont")) \( hat(T) \, hat(T) \) tilde.eq
     hat(T)^d . $ <algebra:tangent-descent>]
  This is an equality obtained from finite presentations; it does not identify ordinary algebraic differentials of
    a power-series ring with its continuous differentials.
  Let $M = "Der"_(bb(C)) \( T \)$. By (6.13), its residue vector space has dimension $d$.
    Lift a basis to $d$ elements of $M$. Nakayama’s lemma gives a surjection $T^d arrow.r M$,
    which after completion is a surjection between free modules of rank $d$ over the local ring $hat(T)$,
    and hence an isomorphism. Faithful flatness of completion annihilates its kernel, proving $M tilde.eq T^d$.
  The ring $T$ injects into its domain completion, so it is a domain. Normality also descends.
    Indeed, if $a \/ b in "Frac" \( T \)$ is integral over $T$,
    it is integral over the normal domain $hat(T)$ and hence belongs to $hat(T)$.
    Thus $a in b hat(T) ∩ T = b T$, where the last equality follows from faithful flatness, and $a \/ b in T$.
    Finally, completion preserves both Krull dimension and the dimension of the maximal ideal modulo its square.
    Equations (6.8) and (6.12) show that $T$ has dimension $d$ and is nonregular whenever $S$ is nonregular.
  The isolated-singularity property can also be retained. Let $frak(j)_(upright("an"))$ be the $d$th Fitting ideal
    of the holomorphic differential module of $S$. The analytic Jacobian criterion identifies its zero set with
    the nonsmooth locus, so some power of the maximal ideal of $S$ is contained in $frak(j)_(upright("an"))$;
    in the smooth case this ideal is the unit ideal. Fitting ideals commute with base change.
    The completed Jacobian presentations and (6.12) thus imply, by contraction from the faithfully flat completion, that
  #[#set math.equation(numbering: n => "(6.14)")
   $ frak(m)_T^b subset "Fitt"_d \( Omega_(T \/ bb(C))^1 \) quad upright("for some ") b gt.eq
     0 . $ <algebra:isolated-descent>]
  On the reduced pure-$d$-dimensional neighborhood constructed below,
    the algebraic Jacobian criterion interprets this containment as nonsmoothness supported at the marked point.
  #emph[An integral normal affine neighborhood with a free tangent module.] The finite module
    $"Der"_(bb(C)) \( A' \)$ localizes at $frak(p)'$ to $"Der"_(bb(C)) \( T \)$,
    because $Omega_(A' \/ bb(C))^1$ is finitely presented. Represent the basis just obtained after inverting an
    element outside $frak(p)'$. It defines a map from a free module of rank $d$ to the derivation module whose
    kernel and cokernel vanish at $frak(p)'$. Their supports are closed,
    so a further principal shrinking makes this map an isomorphism.
  Since $T$ is a domain, exactly one irreducible component of $"Spec" A'$ passes through $frak(p)'$,
    and the support of the nilradical avoids $frak(p)'$. Remove the other components and that support.
    The remaining neighborhood is reduced and irreducible. Its normal locus is open,
    since schemes of finite type over $bb(C)$ are excellent, and contains $frak(p)'$ by the normality of $T$;
    restrict to this normal locus as well. This irreducible neighborhood has dimension $d$,
    since its marked point is closed and its local ring has dimension $d$.
    Its nonsmooth locus is closed and, by (6.14), has no positive-dimensional component through $frak(p)'$.
    Remove any components of that locus that do not contain $frak(p)'$.
  All the restrictions just made are open neighborhoods of $frak(p)'$.
    Choose a principal affine neighborhood contained in their intersection, and write it as $"Spec" A$,
    with marked maximal ideal $frak(p)$. The algebra $A$ is finitely generated, normal and integral,
    its dimension is $d$, and
  $ A_(frak(p)) = T \, wide "Der"_(bb(C)) \( A \) tilde.eq A^d . $
  The marked residue field and completed local ring are unchanged, and the variety is smooth away from that point.
    This proves all the assertions. ◻
]

#proof[
  Proposition 14 supplies a singular normal complex analytic surface germ, smooth away from its marked point,
    whose holomorphic tangent module is free of rank two. Apply Proposition 17 and fix any of the approximations
    and affine neighborhoods furnished by its proof. Its coordinate ring $A$ is a finitely generated normal
    integral $bb(C)$-algebra of dimension two, with
  $ "Der"_(bb(C)) \( A \) tilde.eq A^2 \, wide A_(frak(p)) upright(" nonregular") . $
  Thus the derivation module is free, and in particular projective, whereas $A$ is not regular.
    This is the required counterexample over $bb(C)$. ◻
]

=== 6.3 The smooth locus and derivation blowups <the-smooth-locus-and-derivation-blowups>

Let $X = "Spec" A$ be the affine surface just constructed, and let $U = X_(upright("reg"))$ be its smooth locus.
  Recall that the logarithmic Kodaira dimension of a smooth quasiprojective surface is

$ overline(kappa) \( U \) = kappa \( K_(overline(U)) + D \) \, $

where $overline(U)$ is a smooth projective compactification and its reduced boundary $D =
  overline(U) \\ U$ is a simple normal-crossing divisor. This value is independent of the compactification.

#thm([Corollary 6.3.])[
  #emph[The smooth locus $U$ of the affine surface $X = "Spec"
    A$ constructed in Theorem 1 has $overline(kappa) \( U \) = 2$.]
] <algebra:log-kodaira>

#proof[
  The free tangent sheaf on $X$ restricts to a trivial tangent bundle on $U$.
    If $overline(kappa) \( U \) lt.eq 1$, the theorem of Biswas,
    Gurjar and Kolte [4] Theorem 0.1(1) would make the normal affine surface $X$ smooth, contrary to Theorem 1.
    The only remaining value of logarithmic Kodaira dimension for a surface is two. ◻
]

For a finite $A$-module $M$ of generic rank $r$, viewed as a coherent sheaf on $X$,
  its blowup is universal among proper birational morphisms from integral varieties to $X$ for which the pullback
  of $M$ modulo torsion is locally free of rank $r$. Every such morphism factors uniquely through the blowup.
  We use Villamayor’s description, as recalled in [2] §1.6, Theorem 1.17.

#thm([Corollary 6.4.])[
  #emph[Starting with the singular surface $X = "Spec" A$ of Theorem 1,
    every iteration of blowing up at the derivation module is isomorphic to $X$.
    The same holds if each blowup is followed by normalization. Neither procedure resolves its singular point.]
] <algebra:derivation-blowups>

#proof[
  Since $"Der"_(bb(C)) \( A \) tilde.eq A^2$, the identity of $X$ already has the universal property just stated.
    Its blowup at the derivation module is therefore $X$ itself,
    and the operation remains unchanged at every iteration. Normalization also leaves $X$ unchanged because $A$ is
    normal. The relation with the Lipman–Zariski conjecture was noted in [2] Introduction, after Question 1;
    the surface above gives a negative answer to that question in characteristic zero. ◻
]

